From: Adriel Santos <adriel.turox@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] soc: qcom: smp2p: clear cached inbound values across a restart

qcom_smp2p_notify_in() raises an interrupt only for bits that changed
since the previous notification:

	status = val ^ entry->last_value;
	entry->last_value = val;
	if (!status)
		continue;

The SMEM items, however, outlive the remote processor. When the WCSS Q6
on IPQ5018 is shut down and started again, the firmware republishes the
same ready bit it published on the first boot, last_value still holds it
from that boot, the XOR is zero, and handle_nested_irq() is never
called. qcom_q6v5_wait_for_start() then times out and the root PD start
fails with -110 -- on a processor that booted perfectly well.

Measured on a Xiaomi AX3000T v2 (IPQ5018 + QCN6122): after a clean,
non-crash teardown (wifi down; rmmod ath11k_ahb), the parent smp2p
summary interrupt increments during the failed start, so the Q6 does
kick us, while the "q6v5 ready" interrupt count stays at its cold-boot
value of 1. The doorbell arrives and the edge is swallowed.

Export a helper that drops the cached values for an edge, and call it
from q6_wcss_start() while the Q6 is still held in reset so its next
publish registers as a change. QSDK does the same thing on the SoCs
where it hit this (qcom_clear_smp2p_last_value(), enabled for IPQ5332
and IPQ9574), and mainline's smp2p v2 support clears the same state on
SSR detection.

Signed-off-by: Adriel Santos <adriel.turox@gmail.com>
---
--- a/include/linux/soc/qcom/smem_state.h
+++ b/include/linux/soc/qcom/smem_state.h
@@ -59,4 +59,16 @@
 
 #endif
 
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_QCOM_SMP2P)
+
+void qcom_smp2p_clear_last_value(unsigned int remote_pid);
+
+#else
+
+static inline void qcom_smp2p_clear_last_value(unsigned int remote_pid)
+{
+}
+
+#endif
+
 #endif
--- a/drivers/soc/qcom/smp2p.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/qcom/smp2p.c
@@ -160,9 +160,39 @@
 	struct list_head inbound;
 	struct list_head outbound;
 
+	struct list_head node;
+
 	bool need_ssr_ack;
 };
 
+static LIST_HEAD(smp2p_instances);
+
+/**
+ * qcom_smp2p_clear_last_value() - forget the cached inbound values of an edge
+ * @remote_pid:	processor id of the remote end
+ *
+ * qcom_smp2p_notify_in() raises an interrupt only for the bits that changed
+ * since the previous notification, but the SMEM items outlive a remote
+ * processor's shutdown. A remote that reboots and republishes a value it had
+ * already published therefore produces no edge at all, and whoever waits on
+ * that bit waits forever. Call this while the remote is held in reset so that
+ * its next publish is seen as a change.
+ */
+void qcom_smp2p_clear_last_value(unsigned int remote_pid)
+{
+	struct qcom_smp2p *smp2p;
+	struct smp2p_entry *entry;
+
+	list_for_each_entry(smp2p, &smp2p_instances, node) {
+		if (smp2p->remote_pid != remote_pid)
+			continue;
+
+		list_for_each_entry(entry, &smp2p->inbound, node)
+			entry->last_value = 0;
+	}
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(qcom_smp2p_clear_last_value);
+
 #define CREATE_TRACE_POINTS
 #include "trace-smp2p.h"
 
@@ -651,6 +681,8 @@
 	if (ret)
 		goto set_wake_irq_fail;
 
+	list_add(&smp2p->node, &smp2p_instances);
+
 	return 0;
 
 set_wake_irq_fail:
@@ -680,6 +712,8 @@
 	struct qcom_smp2p *smp2p = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
 	struct smp2p_entry *entry;
 
+	list_del(&smp2p->node);
+
 	dev_pm_clear_wake_irq(&pdev->dev);
 
 	list_for_each_entry(entry, &smp2p->inbound, node)
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_mpd.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_mpd.c
@@ -131,6 +131,15 @@
 
 	qcom_q6v5_prepare(&wcss->q6);
 
+	/*
+	 * The Q6 is held in reset here, so nothing races this. Its SMEM items
+	 * survived the shutdown and it is about to republish the same ready
+	 * bit it published on the previous boot, which smp2p would see as "no
+	 * change" and swallow -- leaving the wait below to time out on a
+	 * processor that actually came up.
+	 */
+	qcom_smp2p_clear_last_value(REMOTE_PID);
+
 	ret = qcom_scm_pas_auth_and_reset(desc->pasid);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(wcss->dev, "wcss_reset failed\n");
